.TH CCM 1 "August 2026" "ccm 0.0.9" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ccm \- monitor CPU, memory, and temperature of multiple hosts via SSH
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ccm
[\fB\-n\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-hostfile\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-H\fR, \fB\-\-hosts\fR \fIHOST\fR[,\fIHOST\fR...]]
[\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-threads\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ccm
is an ncurses-based command line tool that monitors CPU utilization, memory
usage, and CPU temperature of multiple hosts over SSH, displaying real-time
horizontal bar graphs. Both Linux and FreeBSD hosts are supported as
monitoring targets.
.PP
Hosts are queried in parallel using multiple SSH connections. The display
shows the hostname, CPU load, memory usage, and statistics columns
(user/system/nice/idle CPU percentages and temperature). Press
.B q
or
.B ESC
to quit.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-interval \fISECONDS\fR
Update interval in seconds. Defaults to 0.5.
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-hostfile \fIFILE\fR
Use the specified hosts file. Defaults to
.I hosts.txt
with fallbacks to
.I ~/.ccmrc
and
.I /etc/ccm.conf
.
.TP
.B \-H, \-\-hosts \fIHOST\fR[,\fIHOST\fR...]
Monitor the given comma-separated list of hosts. May be combined with a host
file.
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-threads \fIN\fR
Number of parallel SSH threads. Defaults to 4, clamped to the range 1\(en100.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.SH HOSTS FILE FORMAT
The hosts file contains one hostname per line. Lines starting with
.B #
are ignored as comments.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I hosts.txt
Default hosts file, read from the current working directory.
.TP
.I ~/.ccmrc
Per-user hosts file, used if
.I hosts.txt
is not present.
.TP
.I /etc/ccm.conf
System-wide hosts file, used as a last fallback.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
SSH access to the monitored hosts with key-based authentication
.B (BatchMode=yes)
is required; no password prompt can be answered.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (no hosts specified, terminal too small, or privilege
drop failed).
